WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.180
Secure CI/CD for C++ starts with the right mental model.

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:11.640
The pipeline is part of the product's security boundary because it decides what code becomes trusted software.

3
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:19.820
Protect source control, workflow files, release branches, tags, approvals, and the rules that trigger sensitive jobs.

4
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.320
Review the pipeline as an engineering system, not a collection of background scripts.

5
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.360
Control the build path.

6
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:35.980
Isolate runners, clean workspaces, document or pin toolchains, manage caches deliberately, and test the configurations users actually receive.

7
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:45.520
Scope secrets narrowly, keep credentials out of logs and artifacts, protect signing material, and separate production access from workflows that do not need it.

8
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.160
Make release inputs and outputs traceable.

9
00:00:49.260 --> 00:01:03.100
Pin dependencies, verify provenance according to policy, retain build metadata, run layered C++ security checks, generate SBOMs, sign artifacts, and store release outputs in controlled repositories.

10
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.480
Promote known artifacts rather than rebuilding unknown inputs at each stage.

11
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.440
Finally, govern delivery.

12
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:17.180
Use environment gates, approval records, rollback plans, monitoring, and incident response evidence.

13
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.360
Review CI/CD changes as security-sensitive engineering work.

14
00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:30.500
A reliable C++ release process is not just fast automation; it is disciplined automation that can be reviewed, explained, recovered, and trusted.

15
00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.520
Those habits make release speed safer instead of fragile.
